  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/f32cf2ca384f0f1b676e77e74dd4f02484f2ba585710fee88df6951607081a42.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 6+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =12\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{4}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =12\\pi r(\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{in}.$",
